{
  "sections": {
    "hrv": {
      "type": "custom-advertorial-kapoor",
      "blocks": {
        "lede_open": {
          "type": "lede",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>You train five days a week. You sleep seven and a half hours. You’ve cut the second coffee and the midweek drink. Your wearable says your recovery is fine, and fine is exactly the problem - the trend line stopped climbing about eighteen months ago and hasn’t moved since.</p><p>There is a reason for that, and it isn’t your training.</p>",
            "align": "left"
          }
        },
        "head_metric": {
          "type": "section_head",
          "settings": {
            "label": "01 · The metric",
            "title": "HRV isn’t a fitness score. It’s a report on cellular repair.",
            "align": "center",
            "small": false,
            "tight": false
          }
        },
        "lede_metric": {
          "type": "lede",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>Heart rate variability measures the gap between consecutive beats. Wide, irregular gaps mean your nervous system is relaxed and doing maintenance work. Narrow, metronomic ones mean it’s still braced. That’s why HRV drops the morning after a hard session and climbs back once you’ve absorbed it - and why how fast it returns matters more than the number itself.</p><p>The work being reported on is repair, and repair is cellular. Which means HRV is downstream. It’s an output, and like any output it can only be as good as what’s feeding it.</p>",
            "align": "left"
          }
        },
        "metric1": {
          "type": "card_row",
          "settings": {
            "numeral": "",
            "columns": "3",
            "label": "What it reflects",
            "title": "Autonomic balance, and the repair work your body ran overnight.",
            "text": "",
            "dark": false
          }
        },
        "metric2": {
          "type": "card_row",
          "settings": {
            "numeral": "",
            "columns": "3",
            "label": "What moves it",
            "title": "Training load, sleep, alcohol, stress - and the cell’s capacity to do the repair at all.",
            "text": "",
            "dark": false
          }
        },
        "metric3": {
          "type": "card_row",
          "settings": {
            "numeral": "",
            "columns": "3",
            "label": "What it can’t show",
            "title": "Why the capacity itself is lower than it was at 28 years old.",
            "text": "",
            "dark": true
          }
        },
        "head_missing": {
          "type": "section_head",
          "settings": {
            "label": "02 · The missing piece",
            "title": "You’ve optimised every input except the one doing the work.",
            "align": "center",
            "small": false,
            "tight": false
          }
        },
        "lede_missing": {
          "type": "lede",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>Look at what you’ve actually changed over the last two years. Training load, periodised. Sleep, tracked and protected. Alcohol, mostly gone. Protein, creatine, magnesium, vitamin D. Every one of those is a behaviour or a raw material - an instruction sent to the cell, or a delivery made to it.</p><p>None of them changes the cell’s capacity to act on any of it. That’s the layer nobody optimises, because no wearable reports on it and no protocol asks you to. It’s also the layer that determines what the rest of your effort converts into - which is why the trend line can flatten while every input above it is still improving.</p>",
            "align": "left"
          }
        },
        "missing1": {
          "type": "card_row",
          "settings": {
            "numeral": "",
            "columns": "2",
            "label": "Already fixed",
            "title": "The instructions and materials",
            "text": "<p>Training, sleep, diet, the stack. Tracked, adjusted, and about as good as they’re going to get.</p>",
            "dark": false
          }
        },
        "missing2": {
          "type": "card_row",
          "settings": {
            "numeral": "",
            "columns": "2",
            "label": "Still untouched",
            "title": "The machinery that executes them",
            "text": "<p>Mitochondria making energy, repair enzymes clearing damage. Invisible, unmeasured, and declining.</p>",
            "dark": true
          }
        },
        "statement_instruction": {
          "type": "statement",
          "settings": {
            "text": "A better instruction sent to a cell with less capacity to carry it out gets you a smaller result. That is the whole problem."
          }
        },
        "head_cause": {
          "type": "section_head",
          "settings": {
            "label": "03 · The cause",
            "title": "By 40, the fuel that repair runs on has roughly halved.",
            "align": "center",
            "small": false,
            "tight": false
          }
        },
        "lede_cause": {
          "type": "lede",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>NAD⁺ is a coenzyme present in every cell in your body. Mitochondria use it to turn food into energy; repair enzymes need it to fix damage. Without enough of it, the machinery still runs, but slower and with less margin.</p><p>Two systems consume most of it, and both run hardest overnight: energy production, which is non-negotiable, and repair, which is deferrable. They draw on the same pool. When supply is short, energy wins and repair gets what’s left - so a slow HRV recovery is less a nervous system that won’t relax than a repair queue that didn’t get funded.</p><p>NAD⁺ declines steadily with age. By your forties you’re operating on roughly half what you had in your twenties - and training hard raises demand at exactly the point supply is falling. Nothing has gone wrong. You’re doing the same work with fewer resources to recover from it, and the trend line is telling you so.</p>",
            "align": "left"
          }
        },
        "stat": {
