        "faq1": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "I already run a five-product stack. Where does this fit?",
            "answer": "<p>Most of a stack works on inputs your body then has to process - protein, creatine, magnesium, vitamin D. MUTO works a layer beneath that, on the cell’s capacity to use any of it. Doses are printed in full, so overlap is visible rather than guessed at, and the usual outcome is that MUTO replaces two or three separate products rather than adding to them.</p>",
            "open_by_default": true
          }
        },
        "faq2": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Why nicotinamide riboside rather than NMN?",
            "answer": "<p>NR has the larger body of published human research behind it, which is why it is the base of the formula at 300mg. The more important difference is what sits alongside it: pterostilbene extends how long raised NAD⁺ levels last, and BioPerine® improves absorption. A single-ingredient NAD⁺ product leaves that work undone.</p>",
            "open_by_default": false
          }
        },
        "faq3": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How soon will I see it on my wearable?",
            "answer": "<p>There are no stimulants in it, so nothing in the first hour. Most people report the afternoon dip going in weeks three to four; wearable data typically moves between day 30 and day 60. Read it as a trend across weeks, not a single morning’s reading.</p>",
            "open_by_default": false
          }
        },
        "faq4": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What if my data doesn’t move?",
            "answer": "<p>Then you send it back. The 90-day money-back guarantee exists because we are asking you to judge this on your own numbers rather than on how the marketing reads. Log your 30-day HRV average before you start so the comparison is honest.</p>",
            "open_by_default": false
          }
        }
